WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.800
Have you ever wondered why some people
are lactose intolerant? It's because

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.590
their bodies no longer make enough of
an enzyme called lactase, which helps

3
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.930
break down the sugar lactose into
smaller sugars that the body can use.

4
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:15.430
Enzymes are proteins that speed up all
kinds of different chemical reactions

5
00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:15.870
in the body.

6
00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:21.490
Some enzymes, like lactase, help break
things apart, while other enzymes help

7
00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:22.250
put things together.

8
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.280
Let's take a closer look at the
chemical reaction that turns the big

9
00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.040
sugar, lactose, into the smaller sugars,
glucose and galactose.

10
00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.100
The addition of water breaks the bond
in the middle of the lactose molecule

11
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.970
with a chemical reaction called
hydrolysis.

12
00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.430
But the lactose molecule needs to be in
just the right position when it

13
00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.100
encounters the water in order for the
reaction to work. That's where our

14
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.830
enzyme lactase can help. Lactose binds
to a special part of the enzyme called

15
00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:54.770
the active site. We call lactose the
substrate of the enzyme lactase because

16
00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:59.240
it fits into the enzyme's active site,
like a key fits into a lock. Only a key

17
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.050
of the right shape and the right
electric charge will fit. The active

18
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:06.530
site of the enzyme holds lactose in
just the right position to allow water

19
00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:10.400
to come break the bond. Once the
reaction is complete, the enzyme

20
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.100
releases the products of the reaction,
glucose and galactose. Now, the lactase

21
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.490
can find another lactose molecule and
start the process all over again.

22
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.860
Lactase made the chemical reaction
happen faster by holding lactose in

23
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.460
just the right position. When it's
working at full speed, it can split up

24
00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.920
to 60 lactose molecules per second.
People who are lactose intolerant can

25
00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:34.500
actually swallow some lactase before
they drink milk to allow them to break

26
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.640
it down. Thanks to enzymes, all the
chemical reactions in our bodies can

27
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:39.590
happen much faster.

